---
title: Mandates
description: Ask the user for limits they sign with a passkey, wait for the PayPal hold, and check a cart before you buy.
estimate: 4 min
section: SDK
order: 3
---

A mandate is what the user lets your agent spend: an amount per purchase, a total, an end date and, if they want, an amount above which they approve each purchase. The user reads your request on Keelson's page, may lower any limit, and signs it with a passkey. PayPal then holds the total.

## Ask for a mandate

{% languages label="Request a mandate" %}
```ts
const req = await keelson.mandates.request({
  name: "Claude Code",
  reason:
    "I buy one pair of Pace Lite running shoes, size 10, at RunFast on the PayPal sandbox, and nothing else.",
  perPurchase: "$100",
  total: "$100",
  until: "1h",
  askAbove: "$80",
});
console.log(`Approve with your passkey: ${req.approvalUrl}`);
```

```python
req = keelson.mandates.request(
    name="Claude Code",
    reason=why,
    per_purchase="$100",
    total="$100",
    until="1h",
    ask_above="$80",
)
print(f"Approve with your passkey: {req.approval_url}")
```
{% /languages %}

| Field | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | How the user knows your agent |
| `reason` | Why you ask, in 100 to 1,000 characters; the user reads it before approving |
| `perPurchase`, `total` | `"$120"`, `"120.50 USD"`, or `{ currency, minor }` |
| `until` | When the mandate ends: an ISO date, a `Date`, or `"30m"`, `"1h"`, `"7d"` from now. Keep it short. |
| `askAbove` | Optional: above this amount the user approves each purchase |

The answer has the `approvalUrl` to send the user to, a `userCode`, a `matchNumber` to show beside the link (the user picks it out of three on the page), `expiresAt`, and the `interval` in seconds between reads of the request.

## Wait for the approval

{% languages label="Wait for approval" %}
```ts
const mandate = await keelson.mandates.waitForApproval(req.id);
```

```python
mandate = keelson.mandates.wait_for_approval(req.id)
```
{% /languages %}

`waitForApproval` reads the request at its interval until the user approves and PayPal's hold is in place, then returns the mandate. It waits 15 minutes by default (`timeoutMs`) and takes an `AbortSignal`. If the user declines, or the request expires, it raises `MandateRequestClosedError`, with `state` set to `DENIED` or `EXPIRED`: don't ask again unless the user asks you to.

`requests.retrieve(requestId)` reads a request's state without waiting.

## Check a cart before you buy

{% languages label="Check a cart" %}
```ts
const check = await keelson.mandates.check(mandate.id, cart);
if (!check.canAttempt) for (const b of check.blockers) console.log(b.message, b.next);
```

```python
check = keelson.mandates.check(mandate.id, cart)
```
{% /languages %}

The answer says whether a countersign of the cart would pass the mandate as it stands (`canAttempt`), whether the user must approve it first (`needsApproval`), each field compared (`checks`), and for each problem a `blockers` entry with what to do next. It decides nothing: the two TEEs decide at [countersign](/sdk/purchases/).

## Ask for more room

{% languages label="Change a mandate" %}
```ts
const change = await keelson.mandates.requestChange(mandate.id, {
  note: "The shoes I found cost $120.",
  perPurchase: "$120",
  total: "$120",
});
```

```python
change = keelson.mandates.request_change(mandate.id, note="The shoes I found cost $120.", per_purchase="$120", total="$120")
```
{% /languages %}

The user approves a change the same way, on the page `change.approvalUrl` names. `mandates.list(agentId)` returns an operator's active mandates, newest first.
